Case inventory-000: operations handoff review.

The Cedar operations team is preparing a small internal handoff for shift 1. The handoff is read by a coordinator who was not present when the records were created. They need an answer that can be checked against the supplied records without consulting another system. The batch identifier is B0000; it identifies this exercise and does not encode a quantity or a business rule.

Staff keep working notes separately from the transaction records. A note may describe why an item was delayed, where a package was placed, or who should review a discrepancy. Those notes provide context for a human reader. They do not create additional transactions, change a recorded amount, or supply missing values. Only the records and explicit task rules below determine the answer.

The site uses a morning review and an afternoon review. The morning review checks that each entry is legible and that labels match the relevant source document. The afternoon review checks the resulting summary. This question concerns a single frozen snapshot between those reviews. Do not assume another shipment, payment, cancellation, or scan occurred after that snapshot was taken.

The Willow desk receives both routine requests and correction requests. A correction must be represented explicitly in the data before it changes a summary. A coordinator cannot infer a correction from an unusual label or an unexpected total. If the data includes an item more than once, use the stated rules for that task instead of automatically removing duplicates.

The review process favors small, auditable calculations. Intermediate scratch work can be useful to the person doing the review, but the downstream system accepts only the final JSON object. It compares the object with the frozen reference answer. It does not score persuasive explanations, stylistic polish, business recommendations, or guesses about what the records should have said.

All dates and shift labels in this brief are organizational context. There is no exchange-rate lookup, time-zone conversion, tax table, or external calendar rule to apply. Amounts described as cents are integer cents. Counts described as units are whole units. Do not silently convert one unit into another or introduce rounding that the task does not request.

